  "style": {
    "id": "nesting-dolls",
    "name": "Nesting Dolls",
    "description": "A family of lathe-turned, hand-painted, lacquered wooden nesting dolls on a pale birch table in soft window daylight. One lathe profile scaled per level makes every shell (hollow lid + cup with a bare-wood tenon, or solid for the smallest). The big doll's head twists, lifts off with a wooden pop and is set down behind; a smaller doll rises out of the cup and hops aside; the head goes back on; again and again, faster and higher-pitched as they shrink, down to the solid one. Each doll wears the same geometric pattern with less detail than its parent, and a belly emblem of concentric rings — one ring per doll still inside — around a yellow dot. A camera dive into the smallest doll's dot comes out of the biggest doll's dot onto the whole family lined up large to small. One plan (nest.js) drives visibility, every pose, the line-up and the score.",
